(images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-7.$ Therefore, $x=-7$ is a *simple* root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3b22cee7a07af8745945c07bebf63fac213e26dd210b2e41762784fed64a1532.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e919c69-a07f-59d0-9a4f-80f07bde8e75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566201 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=0,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=-5{x}^{2}(x-1)^{2}(x-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67c87009cbafcf40172b4de6cae4cb57e37660dd15235f67500c2a0532d42dee.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13c802b0-f24f-5ef6-be2f-44ec40da3fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566205 #edb/ref 1074 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ has *odd* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d32c9f722bee1a6391c943335c0024be2237aa1779760013fa368508bc8473b6.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38e88fc8-0fa8-5760-aae8-838a940ddec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566220 #edb/ref 1074 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a *simple* root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/75b9d9a0500f526670c59b4f92c28f4da8d979e39c615ae4f25ec960a80ca1e0.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=0$ is a simple root.\n\nIII. $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "571594cb-dae3-572c-bed1-7b71f8315ee0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566229 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=0$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The root $x=0$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
